{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol contains a fatal window between evidence becoming complete in the workspace and its hash being inserted and acknowledged in the distributed Merkle tree. If the process crashes during that window, the orphaned workspace contains completed evidence but the Merkle tree has no inclusion proof for it; the corpse examination therefore finds absence and proceeds to reclaim the workspace, silently displacing evidence that was in fact completed. This directly violates the requirement that the protocol can never silently displace completed evidence.","target":"48afe33acd70f9a567cf4ae020e7e3be5f6b43c1ec98104f5eaca38e655d77b9"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target fails to satisfy the requirement to specify exactly what must be durably recorded. Its mechanism requires both a birth certificate and a redo log capturing every idempotent write so that a new incarnation can reconstruct workspace state and determine completion, yet its claim identifies only the birth certificate as the durable record. By omitting the redo log, the specification is incomplete and does not state the exact set of durable records the protocol must maintain.","target":"7d1ee64a4b2079606baa215f6747e596d55615a3b938502fad115543796a6888"}]}